{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224701","model_call":14,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3615"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3615"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_795520860#4_1606559152","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3615","rank":3,"snippet":"of the past just underneath the new changes. This can be seen in the changes that happened to the inn. The inn has a new owner, and the people there look different, but the painting of King George III still hangs. It is now painted over with a visage of George Washington, but the face of King George III still shows through. This layered portrait reveals the remnants of the past that the new country is based on, suggesting that the past, present, and future are inseparable parts of the same continuity. The Tribulations of Marriage One of the central themes in “Rip Van Winkle” revolves around Rip’s turbulent relationship with his abrasive wife. Rip is an obedient, simple and good man who has to deal with his nagging wife. Irving purposely gives only Rip’s point of view. Rip's <cover>wife, Dame Van Winkle, has no dialogue, and readers are not given her side </cover>of the story.\" , \"start_char\": 1867.000000 , \"title\": \"Rip Van Winkle Themes - eNotes.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.enotes.com/topics/rip-van-winkle/themes\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_795520860#4_1606559152","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":14,"document_judgments":10,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
