{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224701","model_call":55,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"dame van winkle\" (+ sparknotes cliffsnotes shmoop gradesaver) (+ nagging lazy idleness))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"dame van winkle\" (+ sparknotes cliffsnotes shmoop gradesaver) (+ nagging lazy idleness))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_877027687#10_1483985859","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3638","rank":1,"snippet":"from since. Rip Van Winkle tells everyone that for him it has only been one night, which makes them think he is crazy, too. The one piece of good news is that Dame Van Winkle recently passed away. Peter Vanderdonk, the town’s oldest inhabitant, vouches for Rip Van Winkle and says that he has heard tales passed down about the ghosts of Hendrick Hudson and his men appearing once every twenty years; they bowl and keep a guardian eye on the region that Hudson explored. The tale seems to fit with Rip’s experience. Rip goes to live with his daughter, who is married to a cheerful farmer. He lives much happier than he ever was with <cover>Dame Van Winkle. Also, he is now old enough for his idleness to be socially acceptable, and he returns to the hotel and is again well-loved in the village.\" , \"start_char\": 6316.000000 , \"title\": \"Rip Van Winkle and Other Stories “Rip Van Winkle” Summary and Analysis | GradeSaver</cover>\" , \"url\": \"http://www.gradesaver.com/rip-van-winkle/study-guide/summary-rip-van-winkle\" }"},"time":47},"model_calls":55,"document_judgments":39,"relevance_3":27,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
