{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224701","model_call":61,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"dame van winkle\" (+ mean harsh nagging domineering) (+ because rip lazy irresponsible))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"dame van winkle\" (+ mean harsh nagging domineering) (+ because rip lazy irresponsible))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_795508142#13_1606539168","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3641","rank":1,"snippet":"Dutcher Peter Vanderdonk The Self-Important Man Unlock This Study Guide Now \" , \"segment\": \"Most of the older villagers believe Rip's story and credit the existence of Hudson's spirit. Even the younger villagers, who are more skeptical, begin to like Rip. Rip’s story later serves as a comfort to all the men who want to escape their nagging wives. The men are said to wish for a sip of the mystical beer Rip tasted and find themselves freed by a long and deep sleep. Dame Van Winkle<cover>Dame Van Winkle is Rip Van Winkle’s nagging </cover>wife. Rip often tries to escape her contentious nature by spending time with the men outside the inn. He also escapes her by going hunting and fishing. Dame Van Winkle is portrayed as a villain, deserving of blame. Yet, Dame Van Winkle is justified in her nagging, given that Rip won't work the farm or help with domestic matters. Rip leaves Dame Van Winkle alone to fend for the family.\" , \"start_char\": 5446.000000 , \"title\": \"Rip Van Winkle Characters - eNotes.com\" ,","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_795508142#13_1606539168","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":155},"model_calls":61,"document_judgments":44,"relevance_3":29,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
