{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224701","model_call":64,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3642"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3642"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_1089809483#3_2157721122","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3642","rank":4,"snippet":"merit. The comical suggestion that cakes immortalize Knickerbocker reveals that he isn’t well-respected, but also suggests that other forms of immortalization – like getting your face on a penny – are also sort of silly and not all that permanent either. That Knickerbocker enjoyed his research mirrors Rip’s own philosophy of work: that it’s less important for it to be profitable than for it to be done freely and happily. Active Themes Related Quotes with Explanations Knickerbocker’s story opens with a poem by Cartwright about truth. He then proceeds to describe the “magical” beauty of the Catskills. He zeroes in on a small village at the foot of these mountains, where a good-natured man named <cover>Rip Van Winkle lives. Rip’s greatest trouble is his wife, Dame Van Winkle, who is shrewish </cover>and constantly nagging Rip about hisbiggest weakness: that he can find no motivation to engage in profitable labor of any kind.\" , \"start_char\": 1964.000000 , \"title\": \"“Rip Van Winkle” Summary & Analysis | LitCharts\" , \"url\": \"https://www.litcharts.com/lit/rip-van-winkle/summary-and-analysis\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_1089796809#3_2157685693","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_1089796809#4_2157686995","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":64,"document_judgments":47,"relevance_3":32,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
